Specifications

Audio Source: Electric guitar with passive pickups
System: One transmitter and one pocket/belt-worn receiver
Range: < 3m, including movement and body obstruction
Output: Headphone-level output for standard wired headphones
Output connector: 3.5 mm stereo TRS headphone jack (Proposed)
Headphone impedance: 16-64 ohms initial target; verify against actual headphones
Input: 1/4 in TS instrument jack from guitar (Tentative)
Input impedance: 1 MΩ (Target Value)
Input circuitry: Low-noise high-impedance buffer, protection, and adjustable level conditioning (Required)
Audio format: 44.1 kHz, 16-bit uncompressed PCM (Planned)
Channels: Mono, reproduced in both ears (Tentative)
Audio payload rate: 705.6 kbit/s for mono, excluding packet overhead
Wireless technology: Dedicated proprietary 2.4 GHz radio link; no Wi-Fi
Radio bitrate: Evaluate 2 and 4 Mbit/s modes for latency and reliability
Latency: <= 3 ms guitar-input-to-headphone-output target; <= 5 ms initial milestone (To verify)
Controls: Headphone volume, power, and connection status (Planned)
Fault handling: Startup mute, controlled packet-loss handling, and mute on connection loss (Required)
Power: External power for initial bring-up; battery-powered portable operation afterward (Proposed)
Sound: Clean guitar pickup signal; amp/cabinet simulation and effects are optional

Proposed Hardware

MCU and radio: nRF54L15, one per unit
(https://docs.nordicsemi.com/r/bundle/ps_nrf54l15/page/keyfeatures_html5.html)

Audio codec: TLV320AIC3204, one per unit; ADC on transmitter and DAC/headphone drivers on receiver
(https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tac5112.pdf)

Guitar interface: Additional low-noise high-impedance input buffer on transmitter

Clocking: Compatible codec/I2S audio clocks plus firmware correction for transmitter/receiver clock drift

Antenna: Custom 2.4 GHz PCB antenna on each unit

RF circuitry: Nordic package-specific reference matching/filter network, 50-ohm PCB transmission line, and separate antenna tuning footprints

RF test connection: Selectable SMA connection for comparing the custom PCB antenna with an external antenna

Antenna placement: PCB edge with geometry-specific clearance; tune and test with enclosure, battery, headphone cable, and body nearby

Power circuitry: Regulators and decoupling; battery protection and charging for portable version

Debug access: SWD programming connector and power, clock, I2S, and control test points


Signal Path

Transmitter: Guitar -> input buffer/conditioning -> codec ADC -> nRF54L15 -> RF matching/filter -> transmission line -> antenna tuning -> custom PCB antenna
Receiver: Custom PCB antenna -> antenna tuning -> transmission line -> RF matching/filter -> nRF54L15 -> codec DAC/headphone drivers -> headphones

